       "instantiation": "<the passage's objects it is applied at, and the instance that makes it apply>",
       "note": "", "witness": "<why this declaration states the passage>"},
      {"library": "<id>", "declaration": "<full Lean name>", "relation": "hasPart",
       "rank": 2, "confidence": "medium", "clauses": ["c2"],
       "step": "<what this declaration alone entails of the passage>",
       "note": "", "witness": "<why it states that clause>"}],
    "receipt": {"queries": ["<query 1>", "<query 2>", "<query 3>"],
                "candidates": [{"library": "<id>", "declaration": "<name>", "verdict": "accepted", "reason": ""},
                               {"library": "<id>", "declaration": "<name>", "verdict": "rejected", "reason": "<why>"}]}},
   {"item": "<…>", "outcome": "absent", "locations": [],
    "claims": [{"id": "c1", "text": "<…>"}],
    "receipt": {"queries": ["<query a>", "<query b>", "<query c>"], "candidates": [],
                "query_clauses": {"<query a>": ["c1"], "<query b>": ["c1"], "<query c>": ["c1"]}}},
   {"item": "<…>", "outcome": "unresolved", "locations": [], "reason": "<what you could not decide>",
    "receipt": {"queries": [], "candidates": []}}]}
```

- `claims` is required on a `located` and an `absent` passage (and optional on an `unresolved` one): the claim list of step 1. Every location whose relation counts carries `clauses`, the ids it states; a strong relation (`exactMatch`, `closeMatch`, `broadMatch`) names them all. It carries `instantiation` when its declaration takes an instance argument (step 5); a `hasPart` or `narrowMatch` carries `step`, what the declaration alone entails of the passage (step 6), and only those two do; a `closeMatch` carries `convention`, and only a `closeMatch` does. `instantiation` and `step` are one line each, of at least three words of your own: "n/a" is no answer.
- `located`: at least one location whose relation is not `relatedMatch` (with only `relatedMatch` ones, the passage is `absent`); at most {{max_locations}}, ranked 1.. without repeats, best first. `confidence` is `high`, `medium` or `low`.
- `absent`: you searched and nothing in the universe states it, or any clause of it. The receipt lists **at least {{min_queries}} distinct queries** (and at least one per claim) and the candidates you examined, and its `query_clauses` tags every query with the claims it targets, so that each claim has a query of its own (a worked example: one of its own tagged `general` too). An absence may sit beside `relatedMatch` locations only, never while a curated candidate is left unexamined, and never with a candidate rejected for stating part of the passage (step 4).
- `unresolved`: you could not decide (an ambiguous passage, a declaration you could not read), or the passage states nothing a library could state — proof narration or commentary, which PD5 makes no `formal_math` passage: that is the inventory's defect, which you cannot re-scope, so name PD5 in `reason`. Say why in `reason`; its receipt still lists each curated candidate it concerns (step 2). It is counted as unresolved, never as not formalized — prefer it to a guess.
- `coverage` (optional, a `located` passage only): `"coverage": {"complete": true, "parts": [1, 2], "note": "<one sentence>"}` — `parts` are the `rank`s of at least {{min_coverage_parts}} of this passage's `hasPart` locations, whose `clauses` together name every claim, and `note` says in your own words, in one sentence, how they together state every clause (no long quote of the source). Without a claim, `hasPart` locations leave the passage partially formalized however many there are; with one a challenger confirms, the parts count together. Never put `coverage` on an `absent` or `unresolved` passage.
- Copy `item`, the source's shas and `renderer` from the manifest exactly; write no commit and no `proof_status`.
- Write each claim, `note`, query and `reason` in your own words: one that copies more than twelve consecutive words of the article is refused, since the published records quote no more than that.

